lian-iff-contains-commutator-subgroup","used_for":"Direct cited dependency of 5 owned items.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-quotient-module-universal-property","used_for":"Direct cited dependency of 1 owned item.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-reduced-words-form-the-free-group","used_for":"Direct cited dependency of 3 owned items.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-representing-objects-are-unique-up-to-unique-compatible-isomorphism","used_for":"Direct cited dependency of 4 owned items.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-right-adjoints-preserve-limits","used_for":"Direct cited dependency of 1 owned item.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-second-isomorphism-theorem-groups","used_for":"Direct cited dependency of 1 owned item.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-set-has-all-small-colimits","used_for":"Direct cited dependency of 2 owned items.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-subset-of-a-finite-set","used_for":"Direct cited dependency of 1 owned item.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-universal-property-of-module-direct-sums","used_for":"Direct cited dependency of 1 owned item.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-upper-and-lower-central-characterizations-of-nilpotence","used_for":"Direct cited dependency of 1 owned item.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-von-dyck","used_for":"Direct cited dependency of 1 owned item.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-well-ordering-principle","used_for":"Direct cited dependency of 4 owned items.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-yoneda-lemma-is-natural-in-both-variables","used_for":"Direct cited dependency of 2 owned items.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-z-mod-p-is-a-field","used_for":"Direct cited dependency of 7 owned items.","opened":true},{"id":"thm-zorn","used_for":"Direct cited dependency of 1 owned item.","opened":true}],"concerns":[{"id":"def-plus-and-minus-type-of-an-extraspecial-p-group","concern":"The second Remark says the plus/minus signs multiply under central products and cites the odd-prime absorption lemma. Under this item's own odd-p convention, however, M_p is minus and the absorption lemma gives M_p∘M_p≅M_p∘H_p, which is still exponent p² and hence minus. Thus minus central-product minus is minus here, not plus; the Remark is mathematically false as written.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"prop-maximal-elementary-abelian-subgroups-distinguish-the-two-extraspecial-two-groups","concern":"Fact [L17] attributes to def-plus-and-minus-type-of-an-extraspecial-p-group the claim that a 2-group is plus exactly when it is a central product of dihedral factors. The cited Definition instead fixes 2-type by the two solution counts for x²=1; it does not state that central-product characterisation. Step 6 cites [L17], so this is an inaccurate dependency restatement even though [L4] already supplies the count-based distinction needed by the calculation.","severity":"would-be-fatal"},{"id":"prop-a-finite-index-subgroup-of-a-finitely-generated-group-is-finitely-generated-and-quasi-isometrically-included","concern":"Step 1.1 chooses a finite left transversal, but step 3.1 uses the decomposition g=r(g)t(g) with r(g)∈H, which requires representatives of right cosets Ht. Replacing “left” by “right” (or inverting the chosen transversal) repairs the standard Schreier argument immediately.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"thm-classification-of-extraspecial-p-groups-for-odd-p","concern":"Steps 1.3 and 4.1 require the isomorphisms of the peeled factors to induce compatible maps on their common centres. The proof establishes only that C≅C′ and that the peeled factors are both H_p; it does not state the small extra construction that an automorphism of H_p can realize any automorphism of its order-p centre. An explicit scaling automorphism of the Heisenberg model closes the gap.","severity":"gap-a-reader-closes"},{"id":"def-special-and-extraspecial-p-groups","concern":"The first Remark contains a stray proof-ending symbol in the phrase “the form taken ∎ above” inside a definition item.","severity":"presentation"}],"seams_checked":[]}